\section{The interactive console}{

\code{peter()} without a prompt opens a console when someone can answer (interactive R or
IRkernel); \code{peter(.stdin = TRUE)} reads its input from standard input, and
\code{session |> peter()} opens it on \code{session}. With no explicit or configured model, the
interactive console first offers Codex CLI, Claude Code CLI or API configuration
instructions. Choosing an available CLI saves its default model at user scope; cancelling
returns to R. Piped input skips this setup.
Each input is one of:
\itemize{
\item a prompt, sent as \code{session |> peter("...")} with \code{{name}} interpolation; \verb{@file} adds
the head of a file (at most 40 lines) and \verb{@object} attaches an object by name;
\item \code{!code}: R code run in the session's environment; the code and its output go with the next
prompt (at most 300 estimated tokens). \code{!!code} omits that note. Both execute your own R
immediately, without the model's permission gate or a model request. Workspace changes
remain visible to later calls, and enabled transcripts can record the code; \verb{!!} is not a
privacy boundary. A fenced block marked \code{r}, \code{R}, \code{{r}} or unmarked works like \code{!code}
(other fences are prompt text), a \verb{"""} block is a multi-line prompt, and a trailing
backslash continues a line;
\item a slash command such as \verb{/model}, \verb{/mode}, \verb{/cost} or \verb{/exit}; \verb{/help} lists them all.
}

In CRAN's R for macOS in a terminal a pasted block is one input: its lines join the first one
and \code{... } waits for Enter (text typed there is added); a pasted fence or \verb{"""} block is read
up to its closer at once, and a block pasted at an approval or a choice is kept in the console
history and the question asked again. A last pasted line without a newline waits for Enter at
the next prompt, so a two-line paste without a final newline answers with its first line.
Elsewhere (Homebrew or conda R, Linux terminals, RStudio, R.app, Positron, Windows) pasted
lines arrive one at a time; wrap a multi-line paste in \verb{"""}. Lines typed, pasted or run from a
script before a question (an approval, a choice) appeared never answer it: while an answer
runs they are queued for the agent as \code{type_ahead} says (below; a /command or !code line waits
for the next prompt), otherwise they go to the console history. Answer after the prompt.

What you type while peter works, and the key that pauses it, depend on the console:

\tabular{lll}{
\strong{Console} \tab \strong{Typed while peter works} \tab \strong{Pause} \cr
Terminal R on macOS or Linux, an IDE's Terminal tab \tab a draft: Enter steers, Tab sends a
follow-up \tab Ctrl-C \cr
TERM=dumb, Emacs shell, ESS \tab each line, as \code{type_ahead} says \tab Ctrl-C \cr
RStudio, R.app \tab the next prompt, unless a question reads it \tab Esc \cr
Positron \tab R code, run after \code{peter()} returns \tab Ctrl-C \cr
Windows Rterm \tab the next prompt, unless a question reads it \tab Ctrl-C \cr
Windows Rgui, Jupyter \tab the next input \tab none: Esc or interrupt stops \cr
Rscript, pipes, knitr \tab nothing; \code{.stdin = TRUE} reads the pipe \tab none
}

In terminal R and an IDE's Terminal tab what you type while peter works is a draft on the
bottom row, under the transcript, so it never mixes into the answer; a block pasted there is
one message. Enter sends it as a steer: the agent reads it at its next step boundary, when the
response in flight ends after the results of all its tool calls (an answer about to end gets
one more request), and it then shows as your own line. Tab sends it as a follow-up: the next
prompt, sent as its own run once the answer ends. \code{type_ahead = "follow_up"} (\code{\link[=gptr_config]{gptr_config()}})
swaps the two keys. A draft that starts with \code{/} or \code{!} is always a follow-up, so \verb{/command}
and \code{!code} lines run at the next prompt, in the order typed. The bottom row counts what is
queued; Up on an empty draft takes back the newest message not yet delivered, to edit and send
again, and Ctrl-U clears the draft. A question (an approval, a choice) waits until typing
pauses for a second, or three while the draft has text, and the draft stays: through
questions, Ctrl-C and the pause menu, and to the next prompt, where Enter sends it. Keys typed
while R code runs in this process (the \code{r} tool) show when it returns. A steer the answer did
not take (it ended first) is the next prompt; after an abort or an error nothing queued is
sent: the queued text and the draft go to the console history, and Up recalls them. Under
TERM=dumb or in ESS a line typed with Enter is queued as \code{type_ahead} says. On the CLI routes
(\code{claude-cli}, \code{codex}) the agent reads a steer after the CLI's answer, which is often the
whole run.

RStudio, R.app, Positron and Windows consoles hold what is typed until R reads again. In
RStudio, R.app and Windows consoles a line typed while peter works is the next prompt, one
prompt per line, unless a question comes first: it reads the line, queues it as \code{type_ahead}
says and asks again. Positron's console keeps typed text as R code, which runs after \code{peter()}
returns, and clears it at Ctrl-C. To steer in these consoles, pause and press \code{s} (Windows'
Rgui cannot pause). In RStudio an Esc first closes RStudio's own completion list, if open, then
clears a line being typed, so the menu comes at the next Esc. During \code{peter("...")} at R's
prompt, outside the chat, typed lines stay R's next input.

Tab completes only after \code{@} (objects, then files), \verb{@obj$} (its members), \verb{peter$}, a leading
\code{/} (commands) and on a \code{!} or \verb{!!} line (R code), never in plain text: in terminal R, R in an
IDE's Terminal tab, Windows' Rgui and Rterm, and RStudio's console, which completes only what its
popup inserts as offered (the commands after \code{/} and R code after \code{!}, not words holding \code{@} or
\code{$}, which it would wrap in backticks). In a terminal Tab completes the draft the same way while
peter works, and on such a word never sends it. R.app, radian and Positron's console keep their
own completion. gptr never changes RStudio's preferences: as you type in its console, RStudio
adds closing brackets and quotes unless \code{insert_matching} is off, and opens completions unless
\code{console_code_completion} is off (Tools > Global Options > Code).

The pause key opens a pause menu while an answer runs, with the keys \code{s} steer, \code{f} follow-up,
\code{c} continue, \code{a} abort and, for a foreground call (a chat turn included) when available, \code{b}
background. \code{s} and \code{f} ask for the text and queue it as Enter and Tab do; in a \code{peter("...")}
call a follow-up is sent when the agent would otherwise stop. A key typed right after the pause
key answers the menu. Continuing leaves the work as if it had not paused, and pressing the
pause key again aborts. Abort keeps the partial session; what you queued and the draft go to
the history, unsent. Background returns to the chat prompt and keeps the run: what was
queued for after the answer and the draft go to the history, unsent, as after an abort, and
another prompt steers the running session. Nothing runs it while the chat waits for input: use
\code{!!gptr::gptr_wait(gptr::gptr_last())} there to continue it, its answer then shown as a prompt's
and Enter steering it. Under Rscript, background runs advance only inside blocking gptr calls.
Where an interrupt cannot be resumed (Windows' Rgui, Jupyter) the key stops the answer instead.

\verb{/exit}, two consecutive interrupts at the prompt or the end of piped input return the session
invisibly, so \code{session = peter()} keeps the conversation. In a terminal the chat reads keys with
the terminal's echo off and restores it on return, on an error, at an interrupt and at \code{q()};
if R is killed or crashes while peter works, type \verb{stty sane} and press Enter to get it back.

Options: \code{gptr.verbose} (0 silent, 1 progress, 2 streamed console, 3 debug; default 0 in
knitr and testthat, 1 under Rscript, 2 at the console), \code{gptr.max_turns_console}
(turns per console prompt, default 200) and \code{gptr.history} (console inputs to R's history,
default \code{TRUE}). Ordinary progress uses normal output; actual errors use stderr.
\code{vignette("interactive-console", package = "gptr")} gives input examples and the complete
built-in command table.
}

\section{Sessions and results}{

A pipe such as \code{session |> peter("Explain the result.")} adds work to the same session
and kept workspace. Use \code{\link[=gptr_fork]{gptr_fork()}} to branch explicitly. The last turn's answer is
\code{session$text}; \code{session$value} is the newest R value selected by \code{\link[=gptr_return]{gptr_return()}} in any turn,
so a turn that selects none keeps it. Agent-created objects remain in \code{envir}.
}

\examples{
fake = gptr_fake_provider(list("The data has 32 rows.", "The data has 11 columns."))
session = peter("How many rows does the data have?", mtcars, model = fake)
session$text
session |> peter("And how many columns?")
identical(gptr_last(), session)
}
